Vulnerability Title

SQL Injection Due to Dynamic Query Construction Using Untrusted Input

Vulnerability Category
Injection Vulnerability
SQL Injection (SQLi)
OWASP Category (OWASP Top 10 – 2021 / 2025 Mapping)
A03: Injection
CVSS v4.0 Score

CVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:L/AT:N/PR:L/UI:N/VC:H/VI:H/VA:H/SC:H/SI:H/SA:H
Base Score: 9.3 (Critical)

Severity may vary depending on database privileges and exploitability from external interfaces.

Description

During source code review, the application was observed constructing SQL queries dynamically by directly concatenating user-controlled input into SQL statements without using parameterized queries or prepared statements.

The following vulnerable code pattern was identified:

String query = "select * from " + Tbl_TNLocation + 
               " where " + Fld_TNLocationID + 
               " = '" + id_ + "'";

The variable id_ is directly appended into the SQL query string without sanitization or parameter binding.

Additional occurrences of similar insecure query construction patterns were identified across multiple factory classes, including:

TNLocationFactory.java
TNUserFactory.java
TNClientIdentifierFactory.java
TradingNameFactory.java
TNBDBrokerCodeFactory.java

This insecure coding practice allows attackers to manipulate backend SQL queries by injecting malicious SQL payloads.

Impact

Successful exploitation of this vulnerability may allow attackers to:

Bypass authentication and authorization
Extract sensitive database information
Read unauthorized records
Modify or delete database contents
Execute administrative database operations
Perform blind/time-based SQL injection attacks
Potentially achieve remote code execution depending on DB configuration

Impacted data may include:

Trading names
Client identifiers
User records
Business location details
Internal business metadata

If database accounts have excessive privileges, compromise impact may become critical.

Proof of Vulnerable Pattern

Observed insecure query construction:

" = '" + id_ + "'"

Observed use of:

stmt.executeQuery(query)

without prepared statements or parameter binding.

Mitigation
Recommended Fixes
Replace dynamic query concatenation with parameterized queries (PreparedStatement).
Secure Example
String query = "SELECT * FROM TNLocation WHERE TNLocationID = ?";
PreparedStatement pstmt = con.prepareStatement(query);
pstmt.setString(1, id_);
ResultSet rs = pstmt.executeQuery();
Implement strict server-side input validation.
Use least-privileged database accounts.
Avoid exposing raw SQL/database errors.
Perform centralized secure coding reviews.
Use ORM frameworks with safe parameter handling where possible.
Implement Web Application Firewall (WAF) protections for detection.
